WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.320
One of the best things about the home&nbsp;
releases of Marvel movies is that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.800
almost always come with a gag reel.&nbsp;
Here's some examples of Marvel movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.680
bloopers we think might even be&nbsp;
better than the original scenes.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.120
In 2004's Spider-Man 2, Peter Parker&nbsp;
isn't finding young adulthood easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.240
He may have super powers and genius level&nbsp;
intellect, but the challenge of juggling college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.120
multiple jobs, a social life, and fighting&nbsp;
crime as Spider-Man is proving too much for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.560
In one of the many scenes meant to show how Parker&nbsp;
is struggling, he drops his textbooks and as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.520
kneels down to retrieve them, multiple passing&nbsp;
students whack him in the head with their bags.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.320
The movie's gag reel shows us one take&nbsp;
when things went even worse for Parker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.880
although thankfully Maguire has a good sense&nbsp;
of humor about it. Shortly after Parker is hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.240
from behind by one student's bag, he's hit by&nbsp;
another from the front. A prompt shows us the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.960
second student is none other than the director&nbsp;
of the first Spider-Man film trilogy, Sam Raimi.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.000
Although the extended battering of Peter would&nbsp;
have definitely made the finished scene funnier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.200
perhaps Raimi decided that choking his lead&nbsp;
actor with a backpack was going a little too far.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.760
On the Avengers gag reel, Mark Ruffalo&nbsp;
proves himself to either have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.760
wealth of underutilized slapstick&nbsp;
comedy potential or to simply be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.560
the clumsiest actor on just about any&nbsp;
superhero movie set you could find.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.760
In the middle of filming one of his&nbsp;
lab scenes with Robert Downey, Jr.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.600
Ruffalo calls for the crew to wait while he resets&nbsp;
a prop -- which proves easier said than done.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.280
"Careful, actor on set."

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.040
"Sorry. I got it, don't worry!"&nbsp;
It's okay, it's coming!"

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.920
While this clumsiness actually would have&nbsp;
worked well for Bruce Banner's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.520
it may have been a little too silly for&nbsp;
the high-stakes world of the Avengers.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.360
"Dudes, you're on your own!"

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.760
In what the Avengers' Blu-ray calls&nbsp;
"Marvel's First Ever Gag Reel," it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.560
clear that director Joss Whedon's trademark&nbsp;
sense of humor infected the entire cast.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.360
"Stop fighting!"

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.240
But Whedon's not the only one who gets to make&nbsp;
you laugh. As S.H.I.E.L.D. agent Maria Hill, Cobie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.760
Smulders doesn't get to flex her comedy muscles in&nbsp;
the MCU as often she did on How I Met Your Mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.720
However, she makes up for it with what's arguably&nbsp;
the funniest moment in the Avengers bloopers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.920
Pretending to react to the&nbsp;
news of Phil Coulson's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.120
Smulders has what we'll call, a dramatic reaction.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:32.960
"Coulson, no! Oh, God, you were&nbsp;
the greatest man I ever knew..."

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.240
We can't, in good conscience, pretend Maria&nbsp;
Hill's over-the-top meltdown would have fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.920
into the actual movie. But there's no&nbsp;
denying it would have been hilarious.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.840
The first time Captain America, Iron Man, and&nbsp;
Thor meet in The Avengers, things don't go that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.040
well. The thunder god kidnaps Loki from the&nbsp;
Quinjet, prompting Iron Man to fly after him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.960
Intending to follow him, Cap grabs a&nbsp;
parachute and jumps out of the plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.840
But in the gag reel, things&nbsp;
went a little differently.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.040
On the reel, Chris Evans still&nbsp;
grabs the parachute, but has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.840
absolutely no idea how to get the thing&nbsp;
on. He struggles with it for so long that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.520
it gets to the point where you start&nbsp;
to genuinely feel bad for the guy.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.680
"Guess it doesn't go up as&nbsp;
smoothly as I was hoping."

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.360
While it would've taken away from the intensity of&nbsp;
the scene, showing this in the finished film might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.080
actually have made sense for the character. After&nbsp;
all, the last time Steve Rogers used a parachute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.520
was decades earlier, and the design could have&nbsp;
significantly changed since World War II. Seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.760
Steve struggle with his modern parachute could've&nbsp;
been yet another amusing reminder that although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.560
you can take the Captain out of the 1940s,&nbsp;
you can't take the 1940s out of the Captain.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.120
In one of the funnier moments of 2014's Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy, Michael Rooker's Yondu chooses a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.080
unique intimidation tactic. While the Broker&nbsp;
tries to explain why he can't give Yondu the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.120
information he wants, Yondu keeps interrupting him&nbsp;
with gibberish. While this is already humorous in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.320
the movie, in the gag reel, it ventures into the&nbsp;
realm of the absurd, as Rooker just keeps going.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.240
[gibberish]

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.829
“The high end-”

00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:04.585
[gibberish continues]

00:04:04.585 --> 00:04:05.421
“Sir!”

00:04:05.422 --> 00:04:07.680
[gibberish continues again]

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:13.040
Behind him, you can even see co-star Sean&nbsp;
Gunn trying hard not to laugh in a few takes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.960
Sure, we think the finished scene is funny enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.040
but adding in Rooker's extended gibberish&nbsp;
monologue would have taken it to the next level.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.400
Sylvester Stallone and Kurt Russell have a&nbsp;
few things in common. They both hit their fame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:30.000
sweet spots in the 1980s, they both appear&nbsp;
in 2017's Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.200
and both get frustrated on the sequel's gag reel&nbsp;
trying to spit out Guardians-specific names.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.800
The gag reel shows that while&nbsp;
playing the Ravager leader Stakar

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.000
"Ravager.”

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:40.500
“Ravengers."

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.680
"Ravager. What am I saying?"

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:46.720
"Ravenger."

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:47.220
"No!"

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.720
"Ravager."

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:49.600
"Yes!"

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.200
"Ah, forget it, I'll see you in looping."

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.960
Meanwhile, Kurt Russell has&nbsp;
trouble saying his son's codename.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.760
As he camps with the Guardians, Russell says,

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.760
"Well, even where I reside, out&nbsp;
past the edge of what's known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.160
we've heard tell about the&nbsp;
man they call Star Wars."

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.680
Of course, we all know the&nbsp;
name he really means to say is

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.840
"Star Lord."

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:11.380
"Who?”

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.400
"Star-Lord, man. Legendary outlaw?"

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.000
Russell immediately realizes his mistake, and&nbsp;
eventually manages to get his son's name right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.880
However, leaving in the actors' mistakes could&nbsp;
have added a bit to the respective scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.360
Showing Stakar pronouncing "Ravagers" differently&nbsp;
than Yondu could have highlighted the division&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.440
between the two of them. And the fact that&nbsp;
Ego keeps getting his kid's name mixed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.000
with that of a popular Earth movie franchise&nbsp;
could underscore what a horrible father he is.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.960
"Well of course I have issues.&nbsp;
That's my freaking father."

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.960
The scenes set in the dystopian future&nbsp;
of 2014's X-Men: Days of Future Past have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.080
a pretty bleak tone, so it almost feels&nbsp;
wrong that the movie even has a gag reel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.200
But it's good that it does, since&nbsp;
the outtakes are pretty hilarious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.400
Among other funny bits, the gag reel includes a&nbsp;
ridiculous alternate version of a deleted scene.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.680
Before time traveling to the past&nbsp;
to prevent the dystopian future in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.840
which the Sentinels have hunted mutants to&nbsp;
near extinction, Wolverine turns to Storm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.560
After confirming that he'll be the only&nbsp;
one to remember this version of the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.600
he gives his teammate a long kiss. Although&nbsp;
the scene is likely supposed to end there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.120
Halle Berry follows it up with an&nbsp;
unexpected reaction in the gag reel.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:28.720
"Yes! That kiss!"

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.800
Although Berry's response to the kiss would&nbsp;
have broken the bleak mood, the kiss itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.720
may have been a nice addition to the finished&nbsp;
film. On the other hand, sharing a kiss with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.960
Storm earlier in the film might have made seeing&nbsp;
her right before Wolverine's emotional reunion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.800
with the previously deceased Jean Grey at&nbsp;
the end of the film slightly more awkward.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.120
As far as MCU movies go, 2016's Captain&nbsp;
America: Civil War is pretty intense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.160
The final throwdown between&nbsp;
the warring heroes gets ugly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.320
but thankfully one of the architects of&nbsp;
Marvel Comics shows up toward the end to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.600
not only deliver an important package to Tony&nbsp;
Stark, but to make sure we get in a few laughs.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.080
In one of his many beloved Marvel cameos,&nbsp;
the late Stan Lee shows up to the Avengers'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.000
headquarters as a FedEx driver, delivering&nbsp;
the package that contains a letter from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.160
Steve Rogers along with an emergency&nbsp;
contact phone. In the finished product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.400
Lee accidentally misreads the name of&nbsp;
the recipient, much to Rhodey's delight.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.400
"Are you Tony Stank?"

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.560
"Yes, this is Tony Stank. You're in&nbsp;
the right place. Thank you for that."

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.520
However, the film's gag reel shows a&nbsp;
take where Lee says something else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.280
and we're not sure if he did it on&nbsp;
purpose or if it was a legitimate goof.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.520
"Are you Robert Stank?"

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.320
While the original scene was hilarious, fusing&nbsp;
the mispronunciation of the character's last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.880
name with the actor's real-life first name&nbsp;
could've been a fun fourth-wall-breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.920
moment. It even would have made an odd sort of&nbsp;
sense, considering that Marvel has officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.880
canonized the popular fan theory that Lee&nbsp;
is playing a Watcher in all of his cameos.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.400
The blooper reel on the home release for&nbsp;
2016's Deadpool makes it clear that several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.120
of the actors frequently veered off-script.&nbsp;
Deadpool's gag reel includes a wealth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.920
completely improvised lines, particularly from&nbsp;
Ryan Reynolds and his co-star, T.J. Miller.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.440
For example, when Miller's Weasel first sees Wade&nbsp;
Wilson's scarred face, the comedian unleashes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.400
torrent of improvised takedowns, expressing&nbsp;
his disgust at his friend's new appearance.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.720
"You look like somebody turned your face&nbsp;
inside out and just left it like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.480
but then you got in a house fire. God, you look&nbsp;
like a house fire was in another house fire."

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.400
Meanwhile, Reynolds' own improv includes a long&nbsp;
list of jabs toward Gina Carano's Angel Dust.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.720
"Gonna leave me all alone here with Busta Rhymes?”

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.440
“You're gonna leave me all&nbsp;
alone here with Henry Winkler?”

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.320
“Gonna leave me all alone here with HEY YOU GUYS?"

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.200
Hilariously, the reel shows that the line&nbsp;
that made it into the theatrical release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:58.320
ticked Carano off so much she gave Reynolds&nbsp;
an actual punch to the face as a reward.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.040
"Sorry, Gina."

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:01.760
"Mother[bleep]"

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.680
While it's true that putting all of Miller's&nbsp;
and Reynolds' improvised outtakes into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.920
finished film probably wouldn't have worked&nbsp;
from a pacing perspective, we wouldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.960
minded giving each of them an opportunity to riff&nbsp;
just a little bit longer in each of these scenes.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:21.600
One of the most delightful parts of 2018's&nbsp;
Deadpool 2 is Wade Wilson's corrective time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.960
romp during the mid-credits scenes. Deadpool&nbsp;
uses Cable's repaired time travel device to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:32.880
his girlfriend and his X-Force teammate Peter,&nbsp;
prevent the making of the 2011 film Green Lantern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.280
and cut short the action of&nbsp;
2009's X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:41.920
But while Negasonic Teenage Warhead has a&nbsp;
surprisingly easy time fixing the device,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.840
Deadpool has a tough time grabbing ahold&nbsp;
of it. On the gag reel we learn that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.600
took Ryan Reynolds at least four takes&nbsp;
to catch the device without dropping it.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.680
Considering how ridiculous the Deadpool&nbsp;
films tend to be, we can't help but wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.000
if the main character repeatedly fumbling the&nbsp;
time travel device might have actually played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.480
well in the film. Maybe Wade could have even&nbsp;
capped the moment with his signature slogan.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:03.840
"Maximum effort."

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.040
There's one little wonderful nugget about the&nbsp;
making of the Star Wars prequels that fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.520
of the franchise love, even if they aren't&nbsp;
particularly fond of the films themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.000
According to Ewan McGregor, the Obi-Wan Kenobi&nbsp;
actor would regularly make the lightsaber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.560
sound effects with his mouth during fight scenes.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.880
"To begin with, it was quite difficult&nbsp;
not to make the sound. Bzzz. And also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.640
when it goes away, because it makes that&nbsp;
nice shhhhk noise when it goes down."

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.920
Well, according to the gag reel&nbsp;
for 2017's Thor: Ragnarok, McGregor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.600
isn't alone in accidentally trying&nbsp;
to do the sound team's job for them.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.320
Cate Blanchett plays the death&nbsp;
goddess Hela in Ragnarok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.920
and the gag reel includes a few takes&nbsp;
of different scenes in which Blanchett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.920
makes childlike sound effects to accompany&nbsp;
the unleashing of her character's powers.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.800
[Blanchett making noises]

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.000
"I shouldn't make the noises&nbsp;
with my mouth, should I?"

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.160
The gag reel also reveals that Blanchett's&nbsp;
fight scenes with co-star Chris Hemsworth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.800
offered other challenges. The Oscar-winning&nbsp;
actress had a tough time dragging her co-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.280
across the floor, as well as finding&nbsp;
it tough to not hit Hemsworth for real.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:12.480
"Sorry guys, I've been training for&nbsp;
months, but I don't think I can lift that."

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.360
Sure, maybe Hela wouldn't have been&nbsp;
quite as intimidating if she was making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:21.440
her own sound effects and fumbling her&nbsp;
fights with her superpowered siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.040
but in a film filled with plenty&nbsp;
of other visual and audible gags,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.560
including Blanchett's goofs may not&nbsp;
have felt entirely out of place.

